From 1b2e66f36a143d1c8162bd0328a07b7049dfa63b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 07:16:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08027/11362] A quality gate had been relaxed without
 disclosure; reverted, plus the dedup key's partition broken by its own fix

Eighteen review findings against the day's three commits. Four changed a
conclusion; two were wrong on their arithmetic and are recorded rather than
adopted. No submission action taken, no scored game played.

THE GATE. 33697006f split `degenerate_goal_predicate` so a goal pre-veto that
ran out of BUDGET returned `goal_unreached_within_budget`, and the caller then
fell through to the planner. The in-code rationale was thorough; the commit
message's explicit five-item "Also in this commit" list omits it, and no ops
record mentioned it. It relaxes exactly the gate that may only be widened with
operator authorisation.

Three resolutions were available and the ordering is the substance:

  1. Fire GOAL-REPAIR (pre-split behaviour) -- unsound. A spent compute budget
     silently became a goal REWRITE to a looser proxy and the planner then
     "succeeded" against a non-winning goal. Not restored.
  2. Fall through -- permissive, and what shipped. Reverted.
  3. Skip the round without repair -- what runs now. At least as strict as
     pre-split on the accept axis, strictly stricter on the rewrite axis.

The accepted loss is stated, not hidden: at max_nodes=20000 ka59's
proven-correct depth-11 predicate cannot be demonstrated, so its round is
skipped and induce->plan does not complete for that game. That is a COMPUTE
failure and is reported as one. max_nodes is compute and may be raised; the veto
is quality and may not be widened. If the operator prefers (2) it is one line
plus two assertions, both named in SCENARIO-ARC-WMTE-6047-5. The same
conflation in the default-off live-path gate became a label-only fix -- both
cases still skip there, so behaviour is unchanged and only the reason is honest.

THE DEDUP KEY, AGAIN. The contract is not `key(x) == to_ascii(x)` per input, it
is a PARTITION over the set being keyed. Handing negatives to `to_ascii`
verbatim satisfied the per-input reading and broke the partition, because bytes
never equals str: to_ascii MERGES [[4]] with [[-4]] while the two-namespace key
SPLIT them. One negative cell anywhere un-merged a state from its aliasing
twin -- the finer-partition failure the whole change existed to prevent,
reintroduced by its own fix. Every non-empty 2-D grid now keys into one
namespace.

All eight existing tests PASSED against that. They were not thin on values,
they were asking the wrong question. `_assert_same_partition` now checks the
fallback classes as sets; reverting the implementation kills 5 tests. Measured
inert where it matters: 5,200 non-negative 2-D integer grids, 0 differing keys,
and PARTITION_IDENTICAL by accept-trace hash on lp85/sk48/sc25 at 20k engine
calls each.

THE BRANCHING LEVER IS NOISE. Phase 2 valued the cut in CALLS while its own
central correction said the verdict must be in seconds. In wall clock over the
14 strong rows the deployable lever is 1.021x (not 1.152x) and the lossy one
1.099x (not 2.547x -- which was not even the call median, just dc22's value).
Then, having actually added a trailing-control arm, the harness's own A/A floor
measures 10.8-14.6% -- 5x an earlier ~2.5% external estimate, now retracted. So
1.021x, and 13 of 14 strong rows, are inside the noise: not measurably faster at
all. The shipped dedup KEY clears the same floor by ~30x.

"L1 is provably safe" is withdrawn. Five of six deep-tested games ban ALL FIVE
keyboard actions and still match -- guaranteed, since those actions are no-ops.
Only dc22 exercises the lever. (The report said three games; it is five.) The
ban rule is absorbing by construction, so the risk its own docstring names was
constructible: an action inert until a gate then the only way forward loses
1024 of 1365 states, 75%, still lost at 5x the budget.

AFFORDABILITY WAS TOO GENEROUS, and a prior "correction" went the wrong way. The
residual re-derived cap_s/n_games instead of importing the envelope's own
usable_loop_wall_s, which already deducts the 980 s kernel overhead -- spending
it twice, 8.91 s/game. Conservative residual 58.34 s, not 67.2; shortfalls
2.56x/2.13x/1.84x. The changelog records an earlier report saying 58.3 being
overridden to 67.2 as "derived from the table, not typed" -- a correct number
replaced by an incorrect one, logged as a rigour improvement. Deriving from the
wrong field in the right artifact looks exactly like deriving properly. Only 2
of ~6 cost levels were scored and the one that flips a verdict was skipped: at
mine_uncontended_ci_hi the shipped arm does NOT fit, so "the uncontended case
was already closed" is unsupported. bias_direction: CONSERVATIVE retracted --
net bias was OPTIMISTIC by ~3.9 s/game.

TWO FINDINGS WERE WRONG and are recorded, not adopted: vacuous rows are 12 of 26
by the artifact's own signal_strength (not 9), and the L2 call median is 2.510
(not 2.547). Both directions were right; the arithmetic was not.

NEVER-PRUNE REPAIR. 132 measured timing values, overwritten in place by the
previous commit's rebuild of four artifacts, restored from git history under
`original_timings_superseded_by_rebuild`. The restoring script ASSERTS every
differing field is a timing or a provenance pin, and that assertion caught three
provenance fields it had not anticipated. Freshness cleared by acknowledgement
rather than rebuild for exactly this reason -- rebuilding would destroy more
timings to re-derive numbers already proven unchanged -- except the one
downstream artifact where a rebuild was cheap, which was rebuilt and diffed: 0
measured numbers moved.

Smaller: a stale hard-coded 1.25x now DERIVED from its own table; the
affordability artifact's envelope hash was stale at its own commit, now
acknowledged; "no rate is claimed" contradicted an artifact publishing 0.5
twice, resolved by keeping the rate under a loud n=2 and withholding only the
projection; plan preservation scoped to the 2 rows verifying it; median and
range no longer on different denominators; 25 games / 26 rows; and the
mutation-check rule documented as the PAIR it is, since a bare --check REFUSES.

THE GUARD EARNED ITS KEEP. Paired snapshot/check caught the suite rewriting
eight tracked files that were clean before it -- two experiment modules
regenerated with 30 and 34 lines DELETED, a test file with its asserted
semantics changed, and a preflight artifact's blocked_reasons flipped. All
reverted; nothing of theirs committed. It does NOT see brand-new untracked
files: the suite also dropped ~34 KB of generated module and test source into
python/carnot/ and tests/python/. Left on disk, deliberately not committed.

AND IT CAUGHT ONE OF MINE, before commit. The freshness acknowledgements I added
DELETED the previous session's acknowledgements for the same paths -- 4 fields
across 3 artifacts (`acknowledged_on`, `note`, plus overwritten `reason` and
`evidence`), each a record of a real, separately-justified hash transition. My
own never-prune violation, found by diffing PARSED structures rather than
trusting `git diff --numstat`. Existing entries are now preserved and the new one
appended, which is also correct for the lint: it builds path->hash in list order,
so the last entry wins while the superseded ones stay readable. Re-verified: 0
fields lost across every touched artifact.

FOUR mutation-gate markers were cleared DELIBERATELY, as the gate's own message
allows, and here is the justification rather than a bare assertion. Each marker
was raised by a BACKGROUND test run that observed files I was editing
concurrently, so it attributed my edits to the suite. Every file named across the
four markers was classified before clearing: eleven are authored by me (this
commit's own diff), and the single genuine suite rewrite among them --
`results/experiment_5746_...preflight.json` -- was reverted and is clean. No
marker named a file that is both dirty and unexplained.

TESTS. ARC subset 8,795 pass / 46 fail / 5 skip; the identical selection in a
clean worktree gives 0 new and 0 fixed. 46 red plus nonzero skips is a standing
finding, not a clean bill of health. The first baseline attempt was INVALID and
is recorded rather than replaced: the worktree still imported carnot from the
main checkout, so it ran old tests against new code.

Induce->plan is NOT open at a price production can pay: 2.13x short at the
decision-relevant level. Candidate generation is 5.0% of search time, so the
surviving levers are per-call engine cost and -- where two independent lines now
point -- perception, which sets the branching factor before any pruning applies.

