+{
+ "experiment": "outer_loop_arc_branching_factor_corrected_20260730",
+ "title": "Cutting the in-model search's branching factor: what it buys, in seconds, and what it costs",
+ "honest_verdict": "complete_branching_cut_measured_and_devalued_do_not_ship: the deployable lever is worth 1.021x median WALL clock (not the 1.152x its call count suggests), which is DEEP INSIDE the 10.8-14.6% noise floor this session measured -- i.e. not measurably faster at all. Its inert-keyboard half is inductive, has ~1 game of non-vacuous support, and now has a constructed counterexample that permanently loses 75% of reachable states. The high-multiplier variant permanently loses states on 3 of 25 games. Neither closes the conservative production budget. The candidate set is not where the remaining cost is.",
+ "schema": "carnot.outer_loop_arc_branching_factor.v1",
+ "inference_substrate": "aggregation_from_upstream_artifacts",
+ "inference_substrate_principle": "THIS artifact runs no search and calls no engine: it reads the upstream measurement JSONs cited below and recomputes medians, vacuity counts and affordability arithmetic over them. The UPSTREAM runs are offline_arcade_live_agent_runtime_self_discovery_no_llm (CPU-side replay of already-induced engines, no GGUF loaded); declaring that substrate here would be a claim to have done the compute, which adversarial_verify correctly flagged as DURATION_TOO_SHORT against a 0.0 s aggregation duration. Every number is traceable to a cited upstream sha256.",
+ "is_a_solve_claim": false,
+ "why_not_a_solve_claim": "every arm replays engines that already exist and re-derives levels the registry already records. No new level is banked and no game is played online.",
+ "banked_levels": 0,
+ "solve_provenance": "development_proxy",
+ "solve_provenance_principle": "this is the offline dev twin (arc_loop_solve-class replay over environment_files), not the live E3 agent discovering a hidden game. It is a measurement of the search's cost, not evidence the live agent self-discovers anything.",
+ "verifier_is_oracle": false,
+ "read_game_source": false,
+ "used_env_source": true,
+ "offline_ground_truth_bfs": false,
+ "exhaustive_bfs_calibration": false,
+ "hand_calibrated_per_game": false,
+ "question": "the remaining gap to an affordable plan is structural, not tunable: 90.2% of engine calls land on an already-seen grid and 18 candidates are generated per expansion. Can cutting the candidate set close it, and at what cost to completeness?",
+ "WHAT_THE_LEVERS_ARE": {
+  "dedup": "collapse click candidates that resolve to the same point. Safe BY CONSTRUCTION -- it removes duplicate work, never a distinct candidate.",
+  "inert_keyboard": "ban a keyboard action that has been tried and has yielded zero new states. INDUCTIVE, not safe: see the counterexample below.",
+  "object_class": "ban clicks on connected-component classes that have never responded. INDUCTIVE, and measurably lossy.",
+  "L1_deployable": "dedup + inert_keyboard",
+  "L2_lossy": "dedup + inert_keyboard + object_class",
+  "all_three_learn_only_from_the_agents_own_observed_transitions": true
+ },
+ "CORRECTION_1_the_valuation_must_be_in_seconds": {
+  "why": "the cut removes disproportionately CHEAP calls -- the duplicates -- so a call reduction overstates time saved. On ka59 it removes 2.58x of the calls and 1.39x of the wall clock; retained calls are ~2.06x more expensive each. The session report stated this principle as its central correction and then quoted its own headline in calls anyway.",
+  "over_the_14_strong_rows": {
+   "L1_deployable": {
+    "n_rows": 14,
+    "wall_reduction_x_median": 1.021,
+    "wall_reduction_x_range": [
+     1.002,
+     1.21
+    ],
+    "call_reduction_x_median": 1.151,
+    "call_reduction_x_range": [
+     1.007,
+     1.461
+    ]
+   },
+   "L2_lossy": {
+    "n_rows": 14,
+    "wall_reduction_x_median": 1.099,
+    "wall_reduction_x_range": [
+     1.007,
+     1.391
+    ],
+    "call_reduction_x_median": 2.51,
+    "call_reduction_x_range": [
+     1.2,
+     9.437
+    ]
+   }
+  },
+  "as_published_vs_corrected": {
+   "L1_published_x": 1.152,
+   "L1_corrected_wall_x": 1.021,
+   "L2_published_x": 2.547,
+   "L2_corrected_wall_x": 1.099,
+   "note_on_the_2_547_figure": "2.547 is not even the call median -- it is dc22's single value. The call median over the 14 strong rows is 2.510."
+  },
+  "how_many_rows_are_within_noise": {
+   "L1_rows_under_1_08x_wall": 13,
+   "of_n_strong_rows": 14,
+   "the_outlier_the_conclusion_generalised_from": "ka59 at 1.210x wall"
+  },
+  "what_this_does_to_the_conclusion": "'the value is almost entirely in the unsafe lever' does not survive: 1.099x (lossy) against 1.021x (safe). On this evidence NEITHER lever justifies a completeness risk, and the safe one is worth shipping only on expensive engines like ka59."
+ },
+ "CORRECTION_2_the_evidence_is_not_uniform": {
+  "the_claim_as_published": "L1: identical on all 26 games",
+  "what_the_artifact_itself_records": {
+   "rows_total": 26,
+   "distinct_games": 25,
+   "ka59_appears_twice": "once at pinned engine git 341f776c9, once at the results/arc_e3 HEAD engine (the HEAD row is itself vacuous)",
+   "signal_strength_strong": 14,
+   "signal_strength_vacuous": 12,
+   "rows_where_L1_dropped_NOTHING": 7,
+   "rows_with_no_data": [
+    "vc33"
+   ],
+   "rows_where_the_lever_actually_FIRED": 18
+  },
+  "restated": "L1 produced an identical accept sequence on all 26 rows, but the lever only FIRED on 18 of them; 7 rows had it drop nothing at all and vc33 produced no data. By the artifact's own signal_strength field, 12 of 26 rows are vacuous -- their baselines exhaust the queue in 5-148 engine calls and 0.00-0.02 s, so agreement there is near-vacuous and their wall ratios (as low as 0.952x) are pure noise.",
+  "the_review_said_9_of_26_vacuous_which_is_wrong": "the correct figures are 12 vacuous / 14 strong by the artifact's own field, 7 rows where L1 dropped nothing, and 1 row with no data. The direction of the finding is right and the count is not; recorded here rather than repeated.",
+  "why_this_matters": "identity on a row where the lever did nothing is not an observation. The sibling dedup-key artifact handles the same problem explicitly with an EVIDENCE_WEIGHT_IS_NOT_UNIFORM note; the 26-game claim carried no equivalent."
+ },
+ "CORRECTION_3_the_inert_keyboard_lever_is_not_safe": {
+  "the_claim_as_published": "L1 is provably safe / empirically strong across 26 games",
+  "why_the_corpus_evidence_is_vacuous_where_it_matters": {
